<div class="component container col-12">
    <div class="component-content" >

    <div class="component footnotes col-12">
        <div class="component-content">
<div class="cf-footnotes">
    <h5>Footnotes</h5>
        <ol>
                    <li id="cf-fn-1">I&rsquo;m grateful to Edward Knotek, Dani Carlson, and Doug Campbell for assistance with these remarks.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-1" aria-label="Return from footnote 1.">Return to 1</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-2">The Statement on Longer Run Goals and Monetary Policy Strategy as amended effective August 22, 2025, is available via the website of the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System at <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC_LongerRunGoals.pdf" target="_blank">federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC_LongerRunGoals.pdf</a>. Materials from the framework review are available at <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/review-of-monetary-policy-strategy-tools-and-communications-2025.htm" target="_blank">federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/review-of-monetary-policy-strategy-tools-and-communications-2025.htm</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-2" aria-label="Return from footnote 2.">Return to 2</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-3">Find the Cleveland Fed&rsquo;s alternate measures of inflation at our Center for Inflation Research website, <a href="/center-for-inflation-research">clevelandfed.org/center-for-inflation-research</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-3" aria-label="Return from footnote 3.">Return to 3</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-4">See Gerring, Jayme V., Carol Moseley, and Stephan D. Whitaker. 2025. &ldquo;SORCE Insights: Tariff-Related Uncertainty and Pass-Through to Pricing.&rdquo; Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, <em>Cleveland Fed District Data Brief</em>. <a href="https://www.clevelandfed.org/publications/cleveland-fed-district-data-brief/2025/cfddb-20251001-source-insights-tariff-related-uncertainty-and-pass-through-to-pricing">doi.org/10.26509/frbc-ddb-20251001</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-4" aria-label="Return from footnote 4.">Return to 4</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-5">See L&rsquo;Huillier, Jean-Paul, and Martin DeLuca. 2023. &ldquo;The Long-Run Costs of Higher Inflation.&rdquo; Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, <em>Economic Commentary</em> 2023-17. <a href="https://www.clevelandfed.org/publications/economic-commentary/2023/ec-202317-the-long-run-costs-of-higher-inflation">doi.org/10.26509/frbc-ec-202317</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-5" aria-label="Return from footnote 5.">Return to 5</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-6">See Luduvice, Andr&eacute; Victor D., Anaya Truss-Williams, and Christopher J. Walker. 2025. &ldquo;Did Inflation Affect Households Differently? A Look at the Postpandemic Inflation and Wage Growth Dynamics.&rdquo; Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, <em>Economic Commentary</em> 2025-11. <a href="https://www.clevelandfed.org/publications/economic-commentary/2025/ec-202511-did-inflation-affect-households-differently">doi.org/10.26509/frbc-ec-202511</a>. See also Klick, Joshua, and Anya Stockburger. 2024. &ldquo;Examining US Inflation across Households Grouped by Equivalized Income.&rdquo; <em>Monthly Labor Review</em>, July. US Bureau of Labor Statistics. <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.21916/mlr.2024.12" target="_blank">doi.org/10.21916/mlr.2024.12</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-6" aria-label="Return from footnote 6.">Return to 6</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-7">See the Cleveland Fed&rsquo;s most recent Community Issues Survey, <a href="/publications/community-issues-and-insights">clevelandfed.org/publications/community-issues-and-insights</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-7" aria-label="Return from footnote 7.">Return to 7</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-8">Cleveland Fed researchers collect and analyze advance notices filed under the Worker Adjustment and Retraining Notification (WARN) Act. Updates on WARN notices are available at <a href="/research/additional-research-resources">clevelandfed.org/research/additional-research-resources</a>. See Krolikowski, Pawel M., and Kurt G. Lunsford. 2024. &ldquo;Advance Layoff Notices and Aggregate job Loss.&rdquo; <em>Journal of Applied Econometrics</em>. 39(3): 462&ndash;480. <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jae.3032" target="_blank">doi.org/10.1002/jae.3032</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-8" aria-label="Return from footnote 8.">Return to 8</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-9">A variety of approaches provide guidance in this situation. Simple monetary policy rules, including some that are forward looking, usually provide prescriptions for the policy rate as a function of the inflation gap and the output or unemployment gap. Based on the differences in the gaps outlined above, most such rules would call for a restrictive stance of policy compared with its long-run neutral level. For example, see the Simple Monetary Policy Rules section of the Cleveland Fed&rsquo;s website, available at <a href="/indicators-and-data/simple-monetary-policy-rules">clevelandfed.org/indicators-and-data/simple-monetary-policy-rules</a>. Optimal control policies often seek to set the interest rate to minimize the expected discounted future losses coming from the squared inflation gap and the squared output or unemployment gap. A common baseline approach in the context of the FRB/US model assumes that policymakers place equal weights on both inflation and unemployment misses, a placement which again would cause the cumulative expected inflation misses to be a larger factor influencing interest rates given the projected gaps outlined above, but the literature on optimal policy suggests that other weights can be appropriate depending on the model. For example, see Brayton, Flint, Thomas Laubach, and David Reifschneider. 2014. &ldquo;Optimal-Control Monetary Policy in the FRB/US Model,&rdquo; FEDS Notes. Washington: Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, November 21, 2014. <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.17016/2380-7172.0035" target="_blank">doi.org/10.17016/2380-7172.0035</a>. Risk management can also factor into these approaches; for example, see Garga, Vaishali, Edward Herbst, Alisdair McKay, Giovanni Nicol&ograve;, and Matthias Paustian. 2025. &ldquo;Monetary Policy, Uncertainty, and Communications,&rdquo; Finance and Economics Discussion Series 2025-074. Washington: Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.17016/FEDS.2025.074" target="_blank">doi.org/10.17016/FEDS.2025.074</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-9" aria-label="Return from footnote 9.">Return to 9</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-10">See Laubach, Thomas, and John C. Williams, &ldquo;Measuring the Natural Rate of Interest,&rdquo; <em>Review of Economics and Statistics</em>, 85, no. 4 (November): 1063&ndash;1070; working paper version available at <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/pubs/feds/2001/200156/200156pap.pdf" target="_blank">federalreserve.gov/pubs/feds/2001/200156/200156pap.pdf</a>. The New York Fed provides updated estimates from the model at <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/policy/rstar" target="_blank">newyorkfed.org/research/policy/rstar</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-10" aria-label="Return from footnote 10.">Return to 10</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-11">In addition to r-star, this model includes a number of other long-run trend concepts related to the unemployment rate, productivity growth, and price inflation, among others. See Zaman, Saeed. Forthcoming. &ldquo;A Unified Framework to Estimate Macroeconomic Stars.&rdquo; <em>Review of Economics and Statistics</em>. <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.1162/rest_a_01570" target="_blank">doi.org/10.1162/rest_a_01570</a>. For updated estimates, see Horn, Taylor N., and Saeed Zaman. 2025. &ldquo;Neutral Interest Rates and the Monetary Policy Stance.&rdquo; Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, <em>Economic Commentary</em> 2025-08. <a href="https://www.clevelandfed.org/publications/economic-commentary/2025/ec-202508-neutral-interest-rates-and-monetary-policy-stance">doi.org/10.26509/frbc-ec-202508</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-11" aria-label="Return from footnote 11.">Return to 11</a>
                    </li>
        </ol>
</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    </div>
</div>    <div class="component notes">
